  "brief_description": "In this exuberant picture, Rubens seems to suggest that apotheosis -- a person being elevated to divine status -- is not a wholly majestic and dignified affair, as it is presented in many other contemporary paintings. Here, it seems that any great man taken to heaven and granted immortality by the gods has quite a journey ahead of him. George Villiers, Duke of Buckingham, dressed in a seventeenth-century version of Roman armour, is hauled up through the sky in a swirl of moving figures. Minerva, goddess of wisdom and war, and Mercury, messenger of the gods, lead him. His eyes are turned up towards his goal high above: the Temple of Virtue.\n\nThis is a preparatory oil sketch for a painting commissioned for the ceiling of the Duke's residence in London. It outlived him by around 400 years, but both ceiling and portrait were destroyed by fire in 1949 -- only this spectacular picture survives.",
